1. A fuel and exhaust mixing recirculation apparatus comprising:

- a mixing chamber having an exhaust gas inlet, a fuel inlet, and an outlet configured to deliver a fuel-exhaust mixture;
- a nozzle disposed in fluid communication with the fuel inlet and configured to discharge fuel into the mixing chamber;
- a piston assembly including a piston movable within a cylinder in response to exhaust gas pressure communicated from the exhaust gas inlet;
- a valve member operatively coupled to the piston and positioned to regulate fuel flow through the nozzle; and
- a biasing member arranged to urge the piston toward a position corresponding to reduced fuel flow through the nozzle;

wherein an increase in exhaust gas pressure acts on the piston to move the valve member toward an open position, thereby increasing fuel flow through the nozzle and promoting mixing of fuel with recirculated exhaust gas in the mixing chamber, and wherein a decrease in exhaust gas pressure permits the biasing member to move the valve member toward a closed position, thereby reducing fuel flow through the nozzle.

2. The apparatus of claim 1, wherein the piston assembly includes a pressure passage extending between the exhaust gas inlet and a pressure side of the piston.

3. The apparatus of claim 1, wherein the valve member comprises a needle valve received at least partially within the nozzle.

4. The apparatus of claim 1, wherein the biasing member comprises a spring positioned to oppose movement of the piston caused by exhaust gas pressure.

5. The apparatus of claim 1, wherein the mixing chamber is configured to recirculate at least a portion of the exhaust gas to an intake passage of an internal combustion engine.

6. The apparatus of claim 1, wherein movement of the piston varies an effective flow area of the nozzle.

7. The apparatus of claim 1, wherein the piston is mechanically linked to the valve member by a stem extending between the piston and the valve member.

8. The apparatus of claim 1, further comprising an adjustable stop configured to limit travel of the piston and thereby define a maximum fuel flow through the nozzle.

9. A method of regulating fuel flow in a fuel and exhaust recirculation apparatus, comprising:

- receiving exhaust gas under pressure in a mixing chamber;
- applying at least a portion of the exhaust gas pressure to a piston;
- moving the piston in response to the exhaust gas pressure;
- adjusting a valve member coupled to the piston to vary fuel flow through a nozzle; and
- discharging fuel from the nozzle into the mixing chamber to mix with recirculated exhaust gas;

wherein fuel flow through the nozzle is increased as exhaust gas pressure increases and decreased as exhaust gas pressure decreases.

A device comprising:

a body defining a longitudinal axis and including a first passage configured to receive an exhaust gas, a second passage configured to receive a fuel, and a third passage configured to convey an exhaust-gas-and-fuel mixture;

a nozzle cavity formed in the body and including an orifice in fluid communication with at least one of the first passage, the second passage, and the third passage;

a piston slidably disposed at least partially within the body and movable along the longitudinal axis between a first position and a second position, the piston being configured to selectively control fluid communication through the orifice; and

a mixing chamber disposed within the body and arranged to receive exhaust gas from the first passage and fuel from the second passage, the mixing chamber being configured to combine the exhaust gas and the fuel to form the exhaust-gas-and-fuel mixture, the third passage being in fluid communication with the mixing chamber to discharge the exhaust-gas-and-fuel mixture from the body.
